            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Your third -ar verb. By now the pattern should feel automatic — that's exactly what we want. estudiar also hides a nicer feeling than the classroom suggests: at root it means eagerness."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "st-no-e — Spanish props up st- with an e: es-tudiar (the same reflex behind estar, España, escuela). es-too-DYAR."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "diphthong-io — the -iar glides: es-too-DYAR."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "estudiar comes from Latin studēre, \"to be eager, to apply oneself zealously.\" A studium was zeal, enthusiasm, devotion — only later \"study.\" So to study is, at heart, to throw yourself eagerly at something."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That studēre is everywhere in English:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "student — literally \"one who is eager.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "studio, studious, study — the place, the trait, the act."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Italian studio and the musical studio keep the \"devoted practice\" sense."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So Estudio español is \"I devote myself eagerly to Spanish\" — a nicer picture than mere schoolwork."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the template holds",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Drop -ar, add the ending — for the third time:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "person",
                "form",
                "meaning"
              ],
              "columns": 3,
              "rowCount": 3,
              "utterances": [
                "person: I (ending carries the subject). form: estudio. meaning: I study.",
                "person: tú. form: estudias. meaning: you study.",
                "person: usted. form: estudia. meaning: you (formal) study."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three verbs, one pattern: hablo, trabajo, estudio. The singular regular -ar present is now reusable rather than memorized verb by verb."
            }
          ]
        },
